  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to load profile: ${res.status}`);
  const data = await res.json();
  return data.name.toUpperCase();
}
```

The callback loses its binding to the `Timer` instance. `setInterval` invokes the function as a plain function call, so a regular `function () {}` gets its own `this` — `undefined` in strict/module code, or the global object otherwise. Either way, `this.seconds` isn't the timer's `seconds`, so `undefined + 1` (or an equivalent lookup on the wrong object) produces `NaN`.

Fix it with an arrow function, which inherits `this` from the enclosing `start()` method instead of rebinding it:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

Alternative fixes: bind explicitly (`setInterval(function () {...}.bind(this), 1000)`) or capture `this` beforehand (`const self = this;`), but the arrow function is the cleanest option here.

The mutable default argument `DEFAULT_TAGS` is the cause. Python evaluates default arguments once, at function definition time, so every call that omits `tags` shares and mutates the same list. Each test that calls `make_post` without a `tags` argument appends `"post"` to that one shared list, so the list keeps growing across tests and the assertion only holds when this test runs first and alone.

Fix it by giving each call its own list:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = ["draft"]
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

This creates a fresh `["draft"]` on every call, so state no longer leaks between tests.

