```

## Cause

The problem is how `this` works inside a regular `function`. When you use `setInterval(function () {...})`, the function is called as a plain function call, not as a method on your `Timer` object. In a plain function call, `this` does not point to your `Timer` instance — it points to the global object (`window` in browsers, `global` in Node), or `undefined` in strict mode.

So `this.seconds` inside the callback is not the `seconds` property you set in the constructor. It's `undefined` on the global object. `undefined + 1` equals `NaN`, and that's what gets logged every second.

## Fix

Use an arrow function instead of a regular function for the callback. Arrow functions don't have their own `this` — they use the `this` of the code around them, which in this case is the `Timer` instance.

